{
  /* The fleet dashboard's browser client. Bundler resolution and the DOM libs,
     because vite is what builds this and a browser is what runs it — the same
     shape as src/web/tsconfig.json, and for the same reasons.

     WHY THIS PROJECT HAD TO EXIST. The root tsconfig.json includes `tools`,
     which expands to every `.ts` and `.tsx` beneath it. Those flags are
     `nodenext` resolution, `lib: ES2022` with no DOM, and no `jsx` at all —
     so every file in this directory failed there on sight: TS17004 on the first
     angle bracket, and `document` undefined. The root project now excludes
     `tools/fleet/web` and this one claims it instead.

     "exclude" is stated explicitly and must stay stated: tsconfig.base.json
     deliberately carries no file list, but if one ever crept back in it would
     be inherited here and could silently empty this project. `npm run
     typecheck` fails a project that resolves zero files, which is the real
     guard — see scripts/typecheck.ts, and docs/reusable/silent-success.md for
     the family of bug it belongs to.

     No `@/` alias. The product's client has one because shadcn generates
     imports against it; this tool has none deliberately.

     THAT USED TO SAY "since the only thing the alias could reach is `src/`, and
     a tool that must run with the product's server absent has no business
     importing from there", AND SINCE 2026-09-08 THE SECOND HALF IS NO LONGER
     TRUE. Greg asked for the product's voice dictation to be **reused** rather
     than copied, and there are ~3,000 lines of browser audio machinery behind it
     that took a day of debugging to make believable. So the principle is
     narrowed rather than dropped: only LEAF, BROWSER-ONLY, PRODUCT-AGNOSTIC
     modules may be imported from `src/`, nothing that reaches the database, an
     auth session, a slug, an article or a route. `tests/fleet-imports.test.ts`
     walks the whole transitive graph and fails on the twelfth file; the list is
     the cost of the move if this tool ever earns its own repo, and it is meant
     to be reviewed rather than extended.

     The alias stays absent all the same, because relative paths make the reach
     into `src/` visible at every use site, which is exactly what a narrowed rule
     wants. `../../../../src/web/useDictation.js` is meant to look like what it
     is. */
  "extends": "../../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "types": ["vite/client"]
  },
  "include": ["src"],
  "exclude": []
}
